  }}
  .highlight-box.warn {{
    background: #fef3c7;
    border-left-color: var(--orange);
  }}
  .highlight-box.success {{
    background: #d1fae5;
    border-left-color: var(--teal);
  }}
  .stat-inline {{
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--navy);
  }}
  .comparison-grid {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 20px 0;
  }}
  .comparison-card {{
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 24px;
    text-align: center;
  }}
  .comparison-card.fiction {{
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #dbeafe 0%, #bfdbfe 100%);
    border: 1px solid #93c5fd;
  }}
  .comparison-card.nf {{
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #d1fae5 0%, #a7f3d0 100%);
    border: 1px solid #6ee7b7;
  }}
  .comparison-card .genre-title {{
    font-size: 1.15em;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }}
  .comparison-card .big-num {{
    font-size: 2em;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--navy);
  }}
  .comparison-card .sub-stat {{
    font-size: 0.88em;
    color: var(--text-light);
    margin-top: 6px;
  }}
  .rec-list {{
    counter-reset: rec;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }}
  .rec-list li {{
    counter-increment: rec;
    padding: 14px 18px 14px 56px;
    position: relative;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
    background: #f8fafd;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--blue);
  }}
  .rec-list li::before {{
    content: counter(rec);
    position: absolute;
    left: 16px;
    top: 14px;
    background: var(--blue);
    color: #fff;
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }}
  .rec-list li strong {{ color: var(--navy); }}
  .toc {{
    background: var(--card);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 28px 36px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
  }}
  .toc h2 {{
    border-left: none;
    padding-left: 0;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    font-size: 1.25em;
  }}
  .toc ol {{
    padding-left: 22px;
  }}
  .toc li {{
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    font-size: 0.95em;
  }}
  .toc a {{
    color: var(--blue);
    text-decoration: none;
  }}
  .toc a:hover {{ text-decoration: underline; }}
  .footer {{
    text-align: center;
    padding: 28px;
    color: var(--text-light);
    font-size: 0.82em;
  }}
  @media (max-width: 768px) {{
    .container {{ padding: 16px 12px; }}
    .section {{ padding: 24px 18px; }}
    .header h1 {{ font-size: 1.5em; }}
    .comparison-grid {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; }}
    .chart-wrap.half {{ width: 100%; display: block; }}
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- HEADER -->
<div class="header">
  <h1>Amazon Bestselling Books: Price vs. Rating Trend Analysis</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">A Decade of Pricing, Satisfaction, and Genre Dynamics (2009–2019)</div>
  <div class="meta">Generated {datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y')} &nbsp;|&nbsp; Source: Amazon Bestseller Lists via Kaggle &nbsp;|&nbsp; {N:,} Records</div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- TABLE OF CONTENTS -->
<div class="toc">
  <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#market-overview">Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</a></li>
    <li><a href="#price-rating-correlation">Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</a></li>
    <li><a href="#genre-comparison">Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing and Rating Strategies</a></li>
    <li><a href="#temporal-trends">Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</a></li>
    <li><a href="#top-authors">Dominant Authors on the Bestseller List</a></li>
    <li><a href="#recommendations">Strategic Recommendations</a></li>
  </ol>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: MARKET OVERVIEW -->
<div class="section" id="market-overview">
  <h2>1. Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</h2>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{N:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Bestseller Appearances</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card alt">
      <div class="kpi-value">{unique_books}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Unique Book Titles</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card warm">
      <div class="kpi-value">{unique_authors}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Unique Authors</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(avg_rating)}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Average User Rating</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card alt">
      <div class="kpi-value">${fmt(avg_price)}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Average Price</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card warm">
      <div class="kpi-value">{total_reviews:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Reviews</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>Across the 11-year window, the Amazon bestseller list captured <span class="stat-inline">{N:,}</span> annual appearances from <span class="stat-inline">{unique_books}</span> distinct titles by <span class="stat-inline">{unique_authors}</span> authors. The average bestselling book is rated <span class="stat-inline">{fmt(avg_rating)}</span> out of 5 and priced at <span class="stat-inline">${fmt(avg_price)}</span>.</p>

  <h3>Genre Distribution</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Genre</th><th>Appearances</th><th>Share</th><th>Unique Titles</th><th>Avg Rating</th><th>Avg Price</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge badge-fiction">Fiction</span></td>
      <td>{n_fiction}</td>
      <td>{pct(n_fiction, N)}</td>
      <td>{len(set(r['name'] for r in fiction_rows))}</td>
      <td>{fmt(fiction_avg_rating)}</td>
      <td>${fmt(fiction_avg_price)}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge badge-nf">Non Fiction</span></td>
      <td>{n_nonfiction}</td>
      <td>{pct(n_nonfiction, N)}</td>
      <td>{len(set(r['name'] for r in nonfiction_rows))}</td>
      <td>{fmt(nf_avg_rating)}</td>
      <td>${fmt(nf_avg_price)}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Price &amp; Rating Ranges</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Min</th><th>Median</th><th>Mean</th><th>Max</th><th>Std Dev</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Price ($)</td>
      <td>${min_price}</td>
      <td>${fmt(med_price)}</td>
      <td>${fmt(avg_price)}</td>
      <td>${max_price}</td>
      <td>${fmt(stdev(all_prices))}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>User Rating</td>
      <td>{fmt(min_rating)}</td>
      <td>{fmt(median(all_ratings))}</td>
      <td>{fmt(avg_rating)}</td>
      <td>{fmt(max_rating)}</td>
      <td>{fmt(stdev(all_ratings))}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Reviews</td>
      <td>{min(all_reviews):,}</td>
      <td>{median(all_reviews):,.0f}</td>
      <td>{fmt(avg_reviews, 0)}</td>
      <td>{max(all_reviews):,}</td>
      <td>{fmt(stdev(all_reviews), 0)}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Key observation:</strong> Ratings are tightly clustered at the high end (4.3–4.9 for most titles), confirming that bestseller status is a strong quality filter. Price, by contrast, spans from $0 to $105, offering far more variance for analysis.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: PRICE-RATING CORRELATION -->
<div class="section" id="price-rating-correlation">
  <h2>2. Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(price_rating_r, 3)}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Pearson r (All Books)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card alt">
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(fiction_r, 3)}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Pearson r (Fiction)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card warm">
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(nonfiction_r, 3)}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Pearson r (Non Fiction)</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The Pearson correlation coefficient between price and user rating across all {N:,} records is <span class="stat-inline">r = {fmt(price_rating_r, 3)}</span>, indicating a {"negligible" if abs(price_rating_r) < 0.1 else "weak" if abs(price_rating_r) < 0.3 else "moderate"} {"negative" if price_rating_r < 0 else "positive"} relationship. {"Higher-priced books tend to receive slightly lower ratings, though the effect is minimal." if price_rating_r < 0 else "Higher-priced books tend to receive slightly higher ratings, though the effect is minimal."}</p>

  <h3>Scatter Plot: Price vs. User Rating</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrap tall">
    <canvas id="scatterChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Correlation by Genre</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Segment</th><th>Pearson r</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>All Books</td>
      <td>{fmt(price_rating_r, 3)}</td>
      <td>{"Negligible negative" if price_rating_r < 0 and abs(price_rating_r) < 0.1 else "Weak negative" if price_rating_r < 0 else "Negligible positive" if abs(price_rating_r) < 0.1 else "Weak positive"}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge badge-fiction">Fiction</span></td>
      <td>{fmt(fiction_r, 3)}</td>
      <td>{"Negligible negative" if fiction_r < 0 and abs(fiction_r) < 0.1 else "Weak negative" if fiction_r < 0 else "Negligible positive" if abs(fiction_r) < 0.1 else "Weak positive"}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge badge-nf">Non Fiction</span></td>
      <td>{fmt(nonfiction_r, 3)}</td>
      <td>{"Negligible negative" if nonfiction_r < 0 and abs(nonfiction_r) < 0.1 else "Weak negative" if nonfiction_r < 0 else "Negligible positive" if abs(nonfiction_r) < 0.1 else "Weak positive"}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Price Segment Analysis</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Price Segment</th><th>Count</th><th>Share</th><th>Avg Rating</th><th>Avg Reviews</th></tr>
    {" ".join(f'''<tr><td>{seg}</td><td>{d['count']}</td><td>{pct(d['count'], N)}</td><td>{fmt(d['avg_rating'])}</td><td>{fmt(d['avg_reviews'], 0)}</td></tr>''' for seg, d in segment_stats.items())}
  </table>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="segmentChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box warn">
    <strong>Finding:</strong> Price is a poor predictor of customer satisfaction among bestselling books. The near-zero correlation confirms that readers do not rate books higher simply because they paid more. The Budget ($0–$5) segment achieves ratings comparable to Premium ($26+), suggesting that perceived value in bestsellers is driven by content quality, not price.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: GENRE COMPARISON -->
<div class="section" id="genre-comparison">
  <h2>3. Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing and Rating Strategies</h2>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="comparison-card fiction">
      <div class="genre-title"><span class="badge badge-fiction">Fiction</span></div>
      <div class="big-num">{fmt(fiction_avg_rating)}</div>
      <div class="sub-stat">Avg Rating</div>
      <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:1.1em;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">${fmt(fiction_avg_price)}</div>
      <div class="sub-stat">Avg Price &nbsp;|&nbsp; Median: ${fmt(fiction_med_price)}</div>
      <div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:0.9em;color:var(--text-light)">{fmt(fiction_avg_reviews, 0)} avg reviews</div>
    </div>
    <div class="comparison-card nf">
      <div class="genre-title"><span class="badge badge-nf">Non Fiction</span></div>
      <div class="big-num">{fmt(nf_avg_rating)}</div>
      <div class="sub-stat">Avg Rating</div>
      <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:1.1em;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">${fmt(nf_avg_price)}</div>
      <div class="sub-stat">Avg Price &nbsp;|&nbsp; Median: ${fmt(nf_med_price)}</div>
      <div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:0.9em;color:var(--text-light)">{fmt(nf_avg_reviews, 0)} avg reviews</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Side-by-Side Genre Comparison</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Metric</th><th><span class="badge badge-fiction">Fiction</span></th><th><span class="badge badge-nf">Non Fiction</span></th><th>Difference</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Avg User Rating</td>
      <td>{fmt(fiction_avg_rating)}</td>
      <td>{fmt(nf_avg_rating)}</td>
      <td>{fmt(fiction_avg_rating - nf_avg_rating, 3)}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Avg Price</td>
      <td>${fmt(fiction_avg_price)}</td>
      <td>${fmt(nf_avg_price)}</td>
      <td>${fmt(fiction_avg_price - nf_avg_price)}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Median Price</td>
      <td>${fmt(fiction_med_price)}</td>
      <td>${fmt(nf_med_price)}</td>
      <td>${fmt(fiction_med_price - nf_med_price)}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Avg Reviews</td>
      <td>{fmt(fiction_avg_reviews, 0)}</td>
      <td>{fmt(nf_avg_reviews, 0)}</td>
      <td>{fmt(fiction_avg_reviews - nf_avg_reviews, 0)}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Appearances</td>
      <td>{n_fiction}</td>
      <td>{n_nonfiction}</td>
      <td>{n_fiction - n_nonfiction}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Unique Titles</td>
      <td>{len(set(r['name'] for r in fiction_rows))}</td>
      <td>{len(set(r['name'] for r in nonfiction_rows))}</td>
      <td>{len(set(r['name'] for r in fiction_rows)) - len(set(r['name'] for r in nonfiction_rows))}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="genreCompareChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box success">
    <strong>Finding:</strong> {"Fiction" if fiction_avg_rating > nf_avg_rating else "Non Fiction"} holds a slight rating advantage ({fmt(max(fiction_avg_rating, nf_avg_rating))} vs. {fmt(min(fiction_avg_rating, nf_avg_rating))}), while {"Non Fiction" if nf_avg_price > fiction_avg_price else "Fiction"} commands a higher average price (${fmt(max(fiction_avg_price, nf_avg_price))} vs. ${fmt(min(fiction_avg_price, nf_avg_price))}). {"This means Non Fiction readers pay more but rate slightly lower — a value gap worth monitoring." if nf_avg_price > fiction_avg_price and nf_avg_rating < fiction_avg_rating else "The pricing premium in Fiction does not translate into a proportional satisfaction advantage."}
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: TEMPORAL TRENDS -->
<div class="section" id="temporal-trends">
  <h2>4. Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>

  <p>This section tracks how average prices, ratings, and review volumes moved annually from 2009 to 2019 — the most critical lens for understanding market shifts.</p>

  <h3>Average Price Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="priceTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Average User Rating Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="ratingTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Average Reviews Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="reviewsTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Genre Price Trends Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="genrePriceChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Genre Rating Trends Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="genreRatingChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Year-by-Year Data</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th>Avg Price</th><th>Avg Rating</th><th>Avg Reviews</th><th>Total Reviews</th><th>Fiction</th><th>Non Fiction</th></tr>
    {" ".join(f'''<tr><td>{y}</td><td>${fmt(year_stats[y]['avg_price'])}</td><td>{fmt(year_stats[y]['avg_rating'])}</td><td>{fmt(year_stats[y]['avg_reviews'], 0)}</td><td>{year_stats[y]['total_reviews']:,}</td><td>{year_stats[y]['fiction_count']}</td><td>{year_stats[y]['nf_count']}</td></tr>''' for y in years_list)}
  </table>

  <h3>Year-over-Year Changes</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Period</th><th>Price Change</th><th>Rating Change</th><th>Direction</th></tr>
    {" ".join(f'''<tr><td>{pc[0]-1}→{pc[0]}</td><td>${fmt(pc[1], 2)}</td><td>{fmt(rc[1], 3)}</td><td>{'Price ↑ Rating ↑' if pc[1]>0 and rc[1]>0 else 'Price ↓ Rating ↓' if pc[1]<0 and rc[1]<0 else 'Price ↑ Rating ↓' if pc[1]>0 and rc[1]<0 else 'Price ↓ Rating ↑' if pc[1]<0 and rc[1]>0 else 'Stable'}</td></tr>''' for pc, rc in zip(price_changes, rating_changes))}
  </table>

  <div class="highlight-box warn">
    <strong>Inflection point:</strong> {
      # Find the year with the biggest price drop
      max_price_drop = min(price_changes, key=lambda x: x[1])
      max_price_rise = max(price_changes, key=lambda x: x[1])
      max_rating_drop = min(rating_changes, key=lambda x: x[1])
      max_rating_rise = max(rating_changes, key=lambda x: x[1])
      f"The sharpest price decline occurred in {max_price_drop[0]-1}→{max_price_drop[0]} (${fmt(max_price_drop[1])}), while the largest price increase was in {max_price_rise[0]-1}→{max_price_rise[0]} (${fmt(max_price_rise[1])}). The most notable rating drop was in {max_rating_drop[0]-1}→{max_rating_drop[0]} ({fmt(max_rating_drop[1], 3)}), and the biggest rating gain in {max_rating_rise[0]-1}→{max_rating_rise[0]} ({fmt(max_rating_rise[1], 3)})."
    }
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Decade trajectory:</strong> Average bestseller prices moved from ${fmt(year_stats[2009]['avg_price'])} in 2009 to ${fmt(year_stats[2019]['avg_price'])} in 2019 ({fmt(year_stats[2019]['avg_price'] - year_stats[2009]['avg_price'])} change), while ratings shifted from {fmt(year_stats[2009]['avg_rating'])} to {fmt(year_stats[2019]['avg_rating'])} ({fmt(year_stats[2019]['avg_rating'] - year_stats[2009]['avg_rating'], 3)}). Review volumes {"grew substantially" if year_stats[2019]['avg_reviews'] > year_stats[2009]['avg_reviews'] * 1.5 else "remained relatively stable"}, reflecting Amazon's expanding customer base.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 5: TOP AUTHORS -->
<div class="section" id="top-authors">
  <h2>5. Dominant Authors on the Bestseller List</h2>

  <p>Authors who appear most frequently across the decade reveal what sustains bestseller status: series, brand recognition, and cross-genre appeal.</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Author</th><th>Appearances</th><th>Unique Books</th><th>Avg Rating</th><th>Avg Price</th><th>Total Reviews</th><th>Genre(s)</th></tr>
    {" ".join(f'''<tr><td>{i+1}</td><td>{a['author']}</td><td>{a['appearances']}</td><td>{a['book_count']}</td><td>{fmt(a['avg_rating'])}</td><td>${fmt(a['avg_price'])}</td><td>{a['total_reviews']:,}</td><td>{", ".join('<span class=\\"badge badge-fiction\\">Fiction</span>' if g == 'Fiction' else '<span class=\\"badge badge-nf\\">Non Fiction</span>' for g in a['genres'])}</td></tr>''' for i, a in enumerate(top_authors_data))}
  </table>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="authorChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box success">
    <strong>Finding:</strong> {top_authors_data[0]['author']} leads with {top_authors_data[0]['appearances']} appearances across {top_authors_data[0]['book_count']} titles, averaging a {fmt(top_authors_data[0]['avg_rating'])} rating at ${fmt(top_authors_data[0]['avg_price'])}. Dominant authors tend to leverage series (e.g., Diary of a Wimpy Kid, Dog Man) or perennial relevance (e.g., Oh, the Places You'll Go!, The Very Hungry Caterpillar) to sustain bestseller status year after year.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<div class="section" id="recommendations">
  <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>

  <p>Each recommendation below is grounded in a specific finding from this analysis.</p>

  <ol class="rec-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Price competitively in the $6–$15 range.</strong>
      The Budget ($0–$5) and Mid-Low ($6–$10) segments deliver ratings of {fmt(segment_stats['Budget ($0-$5)']['avg_rating'])} and {fmt(segment_stats['Mid-Low ($6-$10)']['avg_rating'])} respectively — on par with or above the Mid ($11–$15) segment at {fmt(segment_stats['Mid ($11-$15)']['avg_rating'])}. Since price does not drive satisfaction (r = {fmt(price_rating_r, 3)}), pricing in the $6–$15 sweet spot maximizes perceived value without sacrificing rating quality. <em>Based on: Price-rating correlation analysis and price segment data.</em>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Avoid premium pricing above $25 unless justified by format.</strong>
      The Premium ($26+) segment averages {fmt(segment_stats['Premium ($26+)']['avg_rating'])} — the lowest rating among all segments — with only {segment_stats['Premium ($26+)']['count']} appearances. High prices on bestseller lists are typically tied to box sets or reference works (e.g., DSM-5 at $105). Standard titles priced above $25 risk both list exclusion and rating penalties. <em>Based on: Price segment analysis and top-author pricing patterns.</em>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Invest in Fiction for satisfaction, Non Fiction for revenue.</strong>
      {"Fiction averages a higher rating (" + fmt(fiction_avg_rating) + ") at a lower price ($" + fmt(fiction_avg_price) + "), while Non Fiction commands a higher price ($" + fmt(nf_avg_price) + ") with a slightly lower rating (" + fmt(nf_avg_rating) + ")." if fiction_avg_rating > nf_avg_rating else "Non Fiction averages a higher rating (" + fmt(nf_avg_rating) + ") at a higher price ($" + fmt(nf_avg_price) + ")."} {"For catalog curation, Fiction delivers better perceived value per dollar; for margin optimization, Non Fiction supports higher price points." if nf_avg_price > fiction_avg_price else ""} <em>Based on: Genre comparison data.</em>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leverage series and children's franchises for repeat bestseller presence.</strong>
      {top_authors_data[0]['author']} and {top_authors_data[1]['author']} dominate the list through recurring series titles. Children's books and series entries achieve both high ratings ({fmt(top_authors_data[0]['avg_rating'])} avg) and repeat appearances, creating compounding visibility. Publishers should prioritize series development and backlist re-promotion. <em>Based on: Top author frequency and rating analysis.</em>
    </li>
